message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: article
title: "Kintsugi: Find and Repair Quantization Damage Where Tasks Break, Not Where Averages Look"
version: "1.1.0"
date-released: "2026-11-24"
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/professorpalmer/kintsugi-method"
authors:
  - family-names: Palmer
    given-names: Cary
abstract: "Low-bit quantization of large language models is usually tuned and reported by an average error, such as the KL divergence to the full-precision model. We report four results from two models that run on one 12 GB GPU, and two corrections that our own checks made necessary. (1) On Qwen3.8-27B, at sampling temperature 1.0, a build at 2.47 bits per weight scores above the BF16 weights on the AppWorld test_challenge split: +32, +24, +35 and +26 task-runs of 417 on four seeds (+117 of 1,668; seed-level p = 5.1e-4, cluster-robust 95 % interval [+51, +183]). At temperature 1.0 the lead is in the weights, not in the setup: the same quantized file in the exact setup of the BF16 run scores 670 against 614 of 834. At temperature 0 (greedy decoding, one run of 417 tasks for each arm) the lead is +11 (330 against 319) and not significant (p = 0.24): BF16 gains about 16 task-runs under greedy decoding and the quantized file does not. So the lead is in how the two files behave under temperature-1 sampling with the same sampler chain. We ruled out the setup, the two-stage output head, drafting and a sharper output distribution; the mechanism is open. The result is from one benchmark and one sampler chain. (2) Average KL does not rank bit maps. Six maps of the same size in the same codec score from 586 to 680 of 834 task-runs, and the two lowest scores belong to maps with 7-9 % lower KL than the shipped map. (3) The nulls. A layer band that class-level KL flagged costs nothing when it alone goes to 2 bits (671 against 670). No single group of layers explains the losses of the lower-KL maps. The losses of a uniform map and of a Hessian-trace map are not significant at the seed level. A repair of the output head lowers KL by 32 % and shows no task effect. A rule from the 27B model (keep the MLP down-projections at 3 bits) does not transfer to a second model for agent work. A sharper output explains none of this. (4) On a looped mixture-of-experts model (Inkling 2 Mini), our 9.07 GB build scores above a public 2-bit file of the same model when the engine, the launcher, the cache types and the reasoning budget are equal: 598 against 579 of 672 task-runs (four of four seeds, seed-level p = 0.019). It is not worse than the official weights. Its 27 % lower KL than that public file comes from the codec, the fits and the engine format: a uniform map at the same bytes keeps 93 % of it. The down projections carry long-range copy margin: the uniform map loses 2.73 nats on copy items, and the public file, which keeps exact down projections in 11 layers, has 2.34 nats more copy margin than our build. We describe a discipline, the Kintsugi method: diagnose with task probes next to KL, repair only the damaged spot, gate every change with lines written before the run, and confirm on data that no choice used. The method found and repaired damage in context: a map with one band restored scores 70 task-runs above the KL-only winner (seed-level p = 0.0017) and is level with the published allocation on agent work at 7.9 % lower KL. In free generation it thinks for 1,184 characters on one deep recall item, where the published allocation and BF16 think for about 210. A forced reading under BF16 shows that this is not damage: both quantized files leave BF16 at the same early tokens, and after that point BF16 itself prefers the long reply. The shipped build keeps the published allocation (L0xRE-V2 by YourHighnessLA) because the repaired map failed a line written before the run, not because damage was shown. The corrections: the task-runs of one seed share one random stream, so the task-level p-values of versions 1.0.x were overstated, and every AppWorld statistic here is at the seed level; and teacher-forced instruments (KL, margins) cannot see free-generation behaviour. We did not invent the quantizer, the trellis codec or the 27B bit allocation."
keywords:
